{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1477662-catalogue-of-weakness-homebrew-anathema-thread","id":1477662,"title":"Catalogue of Weakness: Homebrew Anathema Thread","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Beast: The Primordial"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-03-29T19:20:54-07:00","id":1477662,"title":"Catalogue of Weakness: Homebrew Anathema Thread","content":"The frailties that Heroes can impose on Beasts are an interesting design-space. I thought it might be fun to see what other people have come up with in regards to agency-subverting weak spots (as well as, of course, post my own, such as this one).<br /> <br /> <b>Fugue</b><br /> <br /> A Hero has caused a schism in the Beast\u2019s thoughts and actions, disrupting the synergy between human and Horror. Like Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde, at times the Horror takes over the Beast\u2019s physical body to satiate its Hunger in a fit of antisocial pique. Sometimes Heroes impose this Anathema by \u201cdiscovering\u201d that the Beast is actually the victim of demonic possession, or otherwise conceptualizing the Horror as an alien intelligence highjacking the human form\u2019s will.<br /> <br /> Beasts suffering from this Anathema find themselves stuck between Scylla and Charybdis (an even more uncomfortable truth when their Horror is in fact one of the two famous water-dwellers). At High Satiety their Horror can easily wrest control from their daily lives and cause all sorts of havoc, but Low Satiety brings with it a set of similar problems, as the Horror lingers closer to the surface and makes its wants known in other ways.<br /> <br /> <b>High Satiety:</b> The well-fed Horror has a tendency to \u201csleepwalk\u201d into the Beast\u2019s waking consciousness. In scenes of stress wherein the Beast feels threatened in any way (whether physically, emotionally, or even socially), the player must succeed on a Resolve + Composure roll, penalized by his character\u2019s own Lair rating. On a failure, the Horror takes over his body, acting in primal self-defense and in pursuit of its Hunger. If an Atavism can be used to solve an immediate problem, the Horror will employ it. The Beast regains control at the end of the scene, and remembers nothing of what the Horror has done.<br /> <br /> The personality of the Horror is determined by the player, but always tends toward the, well, bestial. Regardless of whether and how well it can speak, plan, etc, its actions are instinctive, messy, and lacking in consideration for long-term consequences or social niceties. An Eshmaki thinks nothing of vanishing in the middle of a crowded room, and a Tyrant might start ordering others around regardless of context. Even the Inguma, as predisposed as they are toward subtle infiltration, will take on a sociopathic bent, manipulating the unwary according to a plan that only makes sense in that moment to their labyrinthine mind. The Horror cannot access Nightmares while in this state, and only spends Satiety or Willpower with the player\u2019s permission.<br /> <br /> When the Beast merges with his Horror inside the Lair, the Horror takes control automatically, though the player may at any time spend a point of Willpower and make the above-mentioned roll to take the reins for a number of turns equal to successes rolled.<br /> <br /> <b>Middle Satiety: </b>As the Horror stirs in restlessness, its grip on the Beast\u2019s consciousness starts to slacken. The roll to resist its influence is no longer penalized by Lair, and the Beast retains some distorted memory of what happened while under. In addition, while merged, the Beast does not need to spend Willpower to initiate the attempt to regain control.<br /> <br /> <b>Low Satiety:</b> The Horror loses the focus necessary to maintain direct control for long. While out of control, the Beast can spend a point of Willpower to force himself back into the driver\u2019s seat for his Resolve in minutes. He also always remembers what actions the Horror took in his stead.<br /> <br /> <b>Beat: </b>The Beast loses control to the Horror\u2019s dictates.<br /> <br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The Beast gains and then resolves the Ravenous Condition.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-29T20:48:13-07:00","id":1477664,"title":"","content":"Oh, fun. Let\u2019s see, we have chosen blades and missing scales, banes and phobias, and of course seething rage. What hasn\u2019t been done before? What about a \u201chaunted house\u201d one where it forces the Beast to remain near or inside a object or place. Lamps, houses, or a prison irons are things in stories I\u2019ve heard that the hero finds the monster tied too. On a more practical note, it completely shuts down mobile beasts. Unfortunately, it\u2019s a bit late for me to try and write something up, but I could give it a shot tomorrow if it\u2019s a decent idea. A secondary one I\u2019m not sure how to approach mechanically is a mark or something that reveals the beast\u2019s \u201ctrue\u201d nature as a monster deserving to be killed to anyone who sees them. Maybe something like disquiet?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-03-30T08:50:33-07:00","id":1477687,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TempleBuilder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1477662-catalogue-of-weakness-homebrew-anathema-thread?p=1477664#post1477664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh, fun. Let\u2019s see, we have chosen blades and missing scales, banes and phobias, and of course seething rage. What hasn\u2019t been done before? What about a \u201chaunted house\u201d one where it forces the Beast to remain near or inside a object or place. Lamps, houses, or a prison irons are things in stories I\u2019ve heard that the hero finds the monster tied too. On a more practical note, it completely shuts down mobile beasts. Unfortunately, it\u2019s a bit late for me to try and write something up, but I could give it a shot tomorrow if it\u2019s a decent idea. A secondary one I\u2019m not sure how to approach mechanically is a mark or something that reveals the beast\u2019s \u201ctrue\u201d nature as a monster deserving to be killed to anyone who sees them. Maybe something like disquiet?</div> </div> </div> </div>Have you read Dark Ears 2? There's a Binding Anathema in 10001 Nightmares that works in that way (evoking very consciously the trope of the genie in a bottle). A Tell Anathema could be interesting, causing the Horror to manifest directly in the Beast's flesh; repurposing Conspicuous Appearance from Deviant would make a good template for representing such, I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-06T13:16:22-07:00","id":1483247,"title":"","content":"<b>Tell</b><br /> <br /> Heroes see Monsters. It's part of the job description, and more over, it's easy. You see, Monsters always have tells. The hungry dead have no reflection and are pale. The men in black wear glasses to cover their silted eyes and suits to hide their scales. Demons always smell of smoke and sulfur and have cloven feet. No matter how they hide, a true Hero can always find those monstrous tells.<br /> <br /> A Hero has inflamed the pride of the Horror, causing it to revel in its glory. An afflicted Beast finds that their human body twists to more resemble that of their Horror's. No matter how severe the changes appear, the human remains human, and no actual change in capabilities occurs. The changes always sparks fear in those that notice, as mortals instinctively recognize that the marked is a monster.<br /> <br /> The player determines the exact appearance of the tell, but it is always heavily influenced by the Horror. Moreover, the changes are of something clearly not human. Even the most human looking Inguma Horror will display subtly wrong differences, with those greatly exaggerated by this curse.<br /> <br /> <b>High Satiety</b>: The well-fed Horror succumbs easily to pride, boasting of its true nature to the world and subverting the Beast's mortal form. The form becomes a terrible mix of the human and the Horror. More over, it's unconcealable. Observers automatically take notice of the Beast's unusual characteristics. All social rolls against Mortals are reduced to a chance die, with the exception of Intimidation rolls, which enjoy +2 bonus dice. In Social Maneuvering, all mortals are treated as having a impression of hostile. Heroic Stalking is automatically treated as a Exceptional Success against the Beast.<br /> <br /> <b>Middle Satiety</b>: Though the Horror still boastful, it begins to temper its pride with cunning. In a scene where the Beast's appearance could draw attention, the player may roll Wits + Subterfuge -3 , contested by any observer\u2019s Wits + Composure, to conceal their appearance from them. The Beast gains the Notoriety condition upon a Dramatic Failure. Social rolls against any mortal character who notices his appearance lose the 10-again quality. In Social Maneuvering, worsen their impression of them by one level. In addition, Heroes can sense the impact of the revealed Horror. When tracking the afflicted Beast, Mortals who witness the Beast's tells are treated as if the Horror had fed on their nightmares. When stalking, Heroes take the rote quality against the Beast.<br /> <br /> <b>Low Satiety</b>: The Horror's hunger outweighs its ostentatiousness, and the tells become easy to conceal. As Middle Satiety, but the player simply rolls Wits + Subterfuge. Heroes do not take the rote quality when stalking.<br /> <br /> <b>Beat</b>: The Beast's appearance causes them trouble.<br /> <br /> <b>Resolution</b>: The Beast gains and then resolves the Ravenous Condition<br /> <br /> Edit: Thanks to <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar\">espritdecalmar</a> helping review my proposal.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>To whomever reads this, I hope you have a good day/night. May you be Happy.<br /> <br /> So, I made some Mage Legacies <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1497183-templebuilder-s-new-legacy-thread#post1497183\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>, with some help. They vary in quality, but I hope you take a look at them. Every one contains pieces of me, for better or worse.","edited":{"userid":26736,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","user":"TempleBuilder","date":". 06-06-2022, 10:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26736-templebuilder","name":"TempleBuilder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26736_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1251"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-25T10:52:18-07:00","id":1484975,"title":"","content":"<b>Lairlocked</b><br /> <br /> Monsters stick to their nests, for that is where their power is strongest, and most do not dare range farther from home than strictly necessary. The shark is a fierce predator, but pull it out of the water onto the deck of a ship and watch it flop around pathetically. The vengeful ghost can't leave the confines of the abandoned factory it haunts, so as long as you stay outside the barbed wire fence shutting the place off from the outside world, you're safe.<br /> <br /> A Beast with this Anathema has its freedom of movement severely restricted. Not literally--he can still go wherever he chooses--but when away from the dark places with which his soul resonates, he's dangerously weak, unable to rely on the natural gifts of his Horror. In this way a Hero can keep the Beast confined to its Lair, refusing to come out and feed, a stalemate which the canny take advantage of in order to set greater plans in motion.<br /> <br /> <b>High Satiety:</b> Outside the comfort of his Lair, the Beast cannot access Atavisms at all. The Horror is too withdrawn to lend the human half its strengths. The Beast still has access to Nightmares, Lair Traits, and Merits (unless they list an Atavism as a prerequisite), but he can only call upon Atavisms while merged with the Horror. In addition, places antithetical to his Lair fill him with weakness, inflicting a type of homesickness or maybe just normal sickness; in any scene lacking at least one of the Beast's Lair Traits, the character suffers the Deprived Condition and loses the 10-again quality on all rolls.<br /> <br /> <b>Middle Satiety:</b> The Horror's wakened movements give it the incentive to make brief ventures out of the Lair when the tide is high and the stars are right. The Beast no longer suffers the Deprived Condition in scenes lacking Lair Traits (though he still loses 10-again). In addition, he may access Atavisms so long as at least one of his Lair Traits is present in the scene. Rolls to use his Atavisms suffer a penalty equal to (5 - present Lair Traits).<br /> <br /> <b>Low Satiety:</b> When the Horror's belly is empty enough, it cares not about the confines of home. The Beast no longer suffers a penalty when using his Atavisms in the waking world, though all other effects of the Anathema still apply.<br /> <br /> Beasts experience another curious side-effect of Lairlocked at Low Satiety: If the Horror goes out to feed on its own through nightmares, it will only target individuals in range who are sleeping in a place affected by one of the Beast's Lair Traits. The prospective victim does not have to be exposed to the Trait directly, but it must be present in some capacity nearby. If no qualifying characters are in range, the Horror's feeding is stymied.<br /> <br /> <b>Beat:</b> The Beast's player chooses to fail a roll automatically when away from the Lair or its Traits.<br /> <br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The Beast gains and then resolves the Ravenous Condition.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1423,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","user":"espritdecalmar","date":". 06-25-2022, 01:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1423-espritdecalmar","name":"espritdecalmar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1423_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}